    "name": "Genomics Research",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/health/biotechnology/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian population-genomics projects have developed reference resources for studying genetic variation and interpreting potential disease-associated variants. Iranome and a related population-genetics study demonstrate why Iranian diversity matters, while also showing the limits of treating a research cohort as a national clinical resource.",
    "tags": ["Genomics", "Iranome", "Population genetics", "Exome sequencing", "Research ethics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:29:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:29:01Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Genomics Research and Iranome in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranome, Iranian population-genetics research, and the limits of using reference databases to interpret human genetic variation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "population-reference",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why an Iranian reference resource matters",
        "content": "Genomics research examines genetic variation across many genes or across the genome. For Iran, an important contribution has been building population-specific reference data. The original Iranome publication described whole-exome sequencing of 800 individuals from eight Iranian ethnic groups. Exome sequencing targets protein-coding regions; it should not be described as sequencing every participant’s entire genome. [s1]\n\nThe project addressed an interpretive problem: populations poorly represented in international reference resources may lack adequate local comparison data. Iranome was presented as a resource for understanding variation and supporting precision medicine, rather than as evidence that personalized treatment had already become routine. [s1]\n\nA University of Toronto account describes collaboration involving researchers in Toronto and the Genetics Research Center at Iran’s University of Social Welfare and Rehabilitation Sciences. It explains the intended connection between variation databases and the interpretation of human disorders. Those aspirations distinguish the resource’s potential use from demonstrated improvements in patient outcomes. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "different-research-designs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different datasets answer different questions",
        "content": "A related study published in PLOS Genetics examined genome-wide genotype data from 1,021 individuals after quality control, covering eleven ethnic groups. This was a population-genetics analysis, distinct from the original Iranome exome catalogue. Its findings emphasized both overlapping variation among several groups and substantial internal heterogeneity. [s2]\n\nThe practical implication concerns research design: sample selection and ancestry must be considered when investigating disease-associated variation. A finding in one sampled group cannot automatically characterize everyone in Iran. Nor do genetic similarities map neatly onto language or cultural identity; the study identified cases in which linguistic affiliation and genetic proximity differed. [s2]\n\nThese distinctions also discourage a misleading narrative of a single, uniform 'Iranian genome.' The studies provide reference observations from defined cohorts, with particular recruitment and analytical methods, rather than an exhaustive description of a national population. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "versions-and-governance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Version history, consent, and access",
        "content": "The live Iranome FAQ inspected for this page reports 1,202 sequenced samples. However, its statement about 'eight' ethnic groups accompanies a longer list of group names. Without a dated release history, that page cannot establish when the database changed or reconcile its current wording with the original publication. The published cohort is therefore the historical reference used here. [s3]\n\nThe PLOS study documents written informed consent and ethics approval. Its data-availability statement describes access restricted to population-genetic analyses through a data-access committee. Publication of aggregate results consequently should not be confused with unrestricted access to individual-level genetic data. [s2]\n\nIran’s documented contribution is a stronger empirical foundation for studying regional genetic diversity. Establishing clinical benefit requires additional evidence about how these resources are validated and used in diagnosis and care. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
