  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-11-6",
    "page_path": "/explore/health/public-health/",
    "name": "Public Health",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/health/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Public health in Iran combines community-based prevention, vaccination, chronic-disease programmes, environmental protection, and outbreak response. Its primary-care infrastructure provides an important platform, while differences in exposure, resources, and access require attention beyond national averages.",
    "tags": ["Public health", "Primary care", "Vaccination", "Health equity", "Environmental health", "Disease prevention"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:29:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:29:01Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Public Health in Iran: Prevention and Population Health | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s community health services, vaccination, chronic-disease prevention, environmental risks, and gaps in public-health evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "community-foundation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Prevention close to home",
        "content": "Public health addresses the conditions that shape health across populations, including prevention and early detection. Iran’s distinctive foundation is its community health network. UNICEF’s country brief describes rural health houses staffed by community health workers known as behvarzes, alongside urban workers called moraghebe-salamat. Their responsibilities include maternal and child health and the management of communicable and noncommunicable diseases. [s1]\n\nThis network was developed to improve access for disadvantaged communities and reduce rural–urban differences. Its importance is organizational: prevention can be delivered through a continuing local relationship rather than only during hospital treatment. Infrastructure nevertheless describes an opportunity to provide care, not a guarantee that every person receives the same service. [s1]\n\nWHO’s country reporting recognizes continuing socioeconomic vulnerabilities and health inequities. It describes work with the Ministry of Health and Medical Education to integrate social determinants of health into primary care. Here, 'social determinants' means the circumstances affecting health beyond an individual consultation; the programme’s existence should be distinguished from demonstrated reductions in inequality. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "communicable-disease-prevention",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Vaccination requires surveillance as well as coverage",
        "content": "Iran’s vaccination history includes verification of measles elimination by the end of 2019, as reported in a regional analysis published by the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Elimination is an assessment of transmission within a defined setting, rather than a claim that imported infections can never occur. The analysis emphasizes maintaining both population immunity and high-quality surveillance. [s3]\n\nVaccination coverage and disease incidence answer different questions. Coverage estimates describe the proportion receiving a vaccine; surveillance seeks to identify infections and transmission. A high national estimate therefore does not remove the need to investigate local immunity gaps or suspected outbreaks. [s3]\n\nThe historical series below presents a single, explicitly identified WHO/UNICEF release. It preserves that release rather than combining historical values with newer numbers from another revision. It is useful for understanding the published record, but requires replacement with the latest comparable country series before this page is treated as current. [s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-dtp3",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: estimated DTP3 coverage, 2012–2023, historical 2023 revision",
          "dataset_id": "dtp3-2023-revision",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["dtp3"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Surviving infants vaccinated (%)",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "In this historical release, annual estimates remain between 98% and 99%. The small variation does not establish uniform local coverage; the source flags reporting and target-population concerns."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "chronic-disease-prevention",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Chronic disease changes the prevention agenda",
        "content": "Noncommunicable diseases are conditions such as cardiovascular disease, diabetes, cancer, and chronic respiratory disease. Iran adapted WHO’s Package of Essential Noncommunicable Disease Interventions into IraPEN, connecting prevention and care with the primary-care system. WHO’s historical account places IraPEN within the health transformation programme launched in 2014 and describes its connection with mental-health services. [s6]\n\nThis broadens prevention beyond vaccination. Risk assessment, identifying disease earlier, and maintaining appropriate follow-up become continuing responsibilities. However, an institutional description of medicines or programme design at one point in time cannot establish uninterrupted access or effective control in every district today. The historical WHO account is evidence of the programme’s approach, not a current audit of outcomes. [s6]\n\nPublic nutrition belongs within this agenda, but a national narrative needs evidence about food consumption, affordability, nutritional status, and differences between population groups. Those measures should remain distinct rather than being reduced to a single score. This page does not substitute an unverified national nutrition trend for that missing evidence.",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "environments-and-life-course",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Healthy places and health across life",
        "content": "Public health also extends beyond the health ministry. UNDP identifies urbanization, industrial emissions, and traffic congestion as contributors to Iran’s air-pollution challenge, particularly in major cities such as Tehran. This connects population health with transport, energy, industrial practice, and urban management. An environmental exposure should be assessed through its own measurements, rather than inferred from hospital capacity. [s7]\n\nRegional circumstances also matter for infectious disease. WHO’s country reporting highlights malaria resurgence and a dengue outbreak in 2024, particularly in Sistan and Baluchestan. National averages alone cannot explain such geographically concentrated risks. The same reporting describes efforts to address health inequities through local institutions and primary care. [s2]\n\nThe branches of this public-health guide consequently cover disease prevention, public nutrition, environmental and occupational health, older people’s health, and maternal and child health. Each needs its own evidence about exposures, services, and outcomes. Iran’s community infrastructure provides a useful starting point; judging its contemporary performance requires current, geographically detailed information rather than assuming that programme design proves equal protection. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s7"]
      }
    ],
